===== result/findings/VULN-20261110-001/report.md =====
# VULN-20261110-001 — Unauthenticated account existence oracle (email/username)

**Status:** Confirmed (low impact)
**Severity:** Low
**Host:** `https://sso.ui.com`
**Date:** 2026-11-10
**Authentication:** unauthenticated

## Summary
The SSO search endpoints return a distinguishable HTTP status for registered vs
unregistered identifiers, with no authentication:

- `GET /api/sso/v1/search/email/<email>` → `204 No Content` if the email is registered, `404` otherwise.
- `GET /api/sso/v1/search/username/<username>` → `204` if the username is registered, `404` otherwise.

This allows an unauthenticated attacker to confirm whether a given email address
or username has a Ubiquiti/UI account, which aids credential stuffing, phishing
and account-targeting. A rate limit (`429 RATE_LIMITED`) exists but only slows
enumeration slightly.

`Confirmed-vuln.txt: excluded — account-existence oracles are explicitly excluded by AGENT.md §9.`

## Reproduction
Existing test account A:
```
$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/search/email/tan.tiger5564@maildrop.cc
204
```
Existing test account B:
```
$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/search/email/overrated.chinchilla337@maildrop.cc
204
```
Unregistered address:
```
$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/search/email/definitely-not-registered-9931@maildrop.cc
404
```
No cookies are sent in any request. Bursting requests returns
`429 {"error":"RATE_LIMITED","detail":"Too many requests"}`.

## Impact
Low: leaks account existence only. No credential or profile data is returned
(response bodies are empty). Mitigations: rate limiting present.

## Remediation
Return a constant response (e.g., always `204`/`200`) regardless of existence and
enforce the existing rate limit uniformly, or require authentication for these
lookups.

## Evidence
`evidence/enumeration.txt`

===== result/findings/VULN-20261110-002/report.md =====
# VULN-20261110-002 — Pre-MFA login response discloses account UUID and MFA metadata

**Status:** Confirmed (low impact)
**Severity:** Low / Informational
**Host:** `https://sso.ui.com`
**Date:** 2026-11-10
**Authentication:** pre-MFA (password only)

## Summary
`POST /api/sso/v1/login` with a correct email + password returns HTTP `499`
with `required: "2fa"` and a body that discloses identifiers and MFA metadata
before the second factor is satisfied:

- `user.id` / account UUID (`d8946fad-…`) and `user.default_mfa`
- every active authenticator: its internal `id`, `type` (`email`, `backup_code`),
  `status`, `created`, `last_success`, `blocked_reason`, and for email the
  partially masked address (`ta·········64@maildrop.cc`)
- backup-code authenticator `codes_left`
- a WebAuthn `publicKeyCredentialRequestOptions.challenge` and `rpId`

An attacker who knows only the account password (e.g. from a credential-stuffing
list) learns the account UUID, which MFA methods are enrolled, and how many
backup codes remain, without possessing the second factor.

## Reproduction
```
POST https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"user":"<test-account-email>","password":"<test-password>"}
```
Response: `HTTP/2 499`
```json
{"required":"2fa","authenticators":[
  {"id":"b82c4c7d-…","type":"email","status":"active","email":"ta·········64@maildrop.cc",
   "created":"2025-01-12T17:14:04Z","last_success":"…","blocked_reason":null},
  {"id":"ae514c45-…","type":"backup_code","status":"backup","codes_left":10}],
 "publicKeyCredentialRequestOptions":{"challenge":"…","timeout":60000,"rpId":"ui.com",…},
 "user":{"default_mfa":"b82c4c7d-…","id":"d8946fad-…"}}
```
The session created here is the restricted `UBIC_2FA` cookie (10 min, `.ui.com`,
SameSite=Lax). Verified that this pre-MFA session **cannot** access
`/api/sso/v1/user/self/**` (`401 " is not allowed"`), so this is disclosure only,
not an authentication bypass. JWT forgery attempts (`alg:none`) were rejected.

## Impact
Low: information disclosure that lowers the cost of targeted phishing / MFA
social engineering. No account access is gained.

`Confirmed-vuln.txt: excluded — low-impact metadata disclosure, below notification bar.`

## Remediation
Return only the minimum needed to render the MFA step (e.g. the authenticator
`type` and a fully masked hint). Do not return the account UUID, internal
authenticator IDs, `last_success` timestamps, or backup-code counts until the
second factor is complete.

## Evidence
`evidence/pre-mfa-login-response.txt`

===== result/findings/VULN-20261110-003/report.md =====
# VULN-20261110-003 — 2FA backup-code re-mint accepts CSRF-shaped requests without re-auth

**Status:** Corrected 2026-11-10 (Session 4) — not reproducible at assurance level 1; endpoint enforces L2 re-auth
**Severity:** Low (Informational)
**Host:** `https://sso.ui.com`
**Date:** 2026-11-10
**Authentication:** user A (full session)

## Session 4 re-test correction
The original test was performed while the session was at assurance level 2
(recent password/MFA step-up), which made the endpoint appear to accept the
request without re-authentication. Re-testing after explicitly downgrading to
level 1 (`DELETE /api/sso/v1/user/self/login/level`) gives:

```
POST /api/sso/v1/user/self/2fa/backup
Content-Type: application/json
-> HTTP/2 499
{"error":"L2_REQUIRED","detail":"Please re-authenticate with your password","ERRCODE":"L2_REQUIRED"}
```

The same `L2_REQUIRED` gate was observed for other sensitive self-service
operations (email change request, add email authenticator, password/username
change, picture upload). Therefore the "no re-auth" property described below is
incorrect for a level-1 session; exploitation would additionally require the
attacker to hold a level-2 (re-authenticated) session. Severity is Informational.
The original observations are preserved below for the record.

## Summary
`POST /api/sso/v1/user/self/2fa/backup` generates a fresh set of 10 MFA backup
codes and returns them in the response. Two properties are noteworthy:

1. It requires **no re-authentication** (no password, TOTP, or step-up prompt),
   only the normal session cookie.
2. It accepts `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded` with an empty
   body. That is a CORS "simple request", so no preflight is needed, and the
   endpoint does not require an anti-CSRF token.

Each call invalidates/replaces the previous backup-code set (new codes returned,
`codes_left` reset to 10) while keeping the same authenticator id, so a forged
same-site request can invalidate a victim's saved recovery codes (recovery DoS).

## Exploitability constraints
- The auth cookie is `UBIC_AUTH; Domain=.ui.com; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Secure`.
  A cross-site (`evil.com`) form POST would **not** carry the cookie, so this is
  not exploitable from arbitrary third-party sites.
- It **is** triggerable from any same-site `*.ui.com` origin, and `sso.ui.com`
  reflects any `*.ui.com` Origin with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`,
  so an attacker with content on any `ui.com` subdomain could mint and read the
  victim's backup codes from a browser.
- A stolen full session already grants account access, so the incremental impact
  is persistence/MFA-recovery disruption rather than a standalone takeover.

## Reproduction
With a valid full session cookie:
```
POST https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/user/self/2fa/backup
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Content-Length: 0

HTTP/2 201
{"id":"ae514c45-…","created":"…","last_success":null,"type":"backup_code",
 "status":"backup","codes_left":10,"backup_codes":["cfd3b0-904a06", … 10 codes …]}
```
Repeating the request returns a different code set (same authenticator id),
demonstrating that old codes are replaced without re-auth.

## Negative checks
- `Content-Type: text/plain` → `415 UNSUPPORTED_MEDIA_TYPE`.
- Pre-MFA `UBIC_2FA` session → `401 " is not allowed"` (no MFA bypass).
- No `Origin`/`Referer` enforcement observed for same-site requests.

`Confirmed-vuln.txt: excluded — requires same-site origin control or an already-stolen session; below the high-impact bar.`

## Remediation
Require step-up re-authentication (password or current TOTP/backup code) before
regenerating backup codes, require JSON + a CSRF token, and validate
`Origin`/`Referer`. Consider not replacing existing codes until the new set is
confirmed by the user.

## Evidence
`evidence/backup-remint.txt`

===== result/findings/VULN-20261110-004/report.md =====
# VULN-20261110-004 — Logout CSRF: `GET /api/sso/v1/logout` clears `.ui.com` auth cookies

**Status:** Confirmed
**Severity:** Low (Informational)
**Host:** `https://sso.ui.com`
**Date:** 2026-11-10
**Authentication:** unauthenticated request is sufficient to receive cookie-clearing headers

## Summary
The logout endpoint accepts `GET` (advertised in `Allow: GET, POST, HEAD, OPTIONS`)
and responds with `Set-Cookie` headers that expire the shared `.ui.com` authentication
cookies (`UBIC_AUTH`, `UBIC_2FA`, and `CloudFront-*` signed-cookie values). No CSRF
token, `Origin` check, or session validation is required; the same clearing headers
are returned even when the request carries no session or a syntactically invalid
`UBIC_AUTH` value.

A third-party page can therefore log a victim out of all `ui.com` properties with a
top-level navigation (or a link) to `https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/logout`. The
cookies are `SameSite=Lax`, so a cross-site top-level `GET` includes them and the
browser processes the clearing response; cross-site subresource loads may be blocked
by third-party-cookie policy depending on browser version.

Impact is limited to forced logout / session disruption; it does not leak data or
grant access.

## Reproduction
```
GET https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/logout
```
Response (abridged):
```
HTTP/2 302
location: https://account.ui.com/login
set-cookie: UBIC_AUTH=""; Domain=.ui.com; Max-Age=0; Path=/
set-cookie: UBIC_2FA=""; Domain=.ui.com; Max-Age=0; Path=/
set-cookie: CloudFront-Policy=; Domain=.ui.com; Max-Age=0; Path=/
...
allow: GET, POST, HEAD, OPTIONS
```
`POST` returns `204` and also clears the cookies, but the `GET` method is the
CSRF-relevant issue.

## Negative checks
- No `Origin`/`Referer` validation was observed.
- Response is `Cache-Control: private, max-age=0, no-cache` (not cacheable).
- No user data is disclosed; only logout/DoS impact.

`Confirmed-vuln.txt: excluded — logout CSRF is below the high-impact notification bar (no XSS/CSTI/IDOR/BAC/open redirect).`

## Remediation
Require `POST` with a CSRF token (and/or strict `Origin` validation) for logout;
do not accept state-changing `GET` requests.

## Evidence
`evidence/logout-get.txt`

===== result/findings/VULN-20261111-001/report.md =====
# VULN-20261111-001 — Zendesk comment HTML rendered unsanitized (Unresolved / self-XSS)

**Status:** Unresolved (defense gap confirmed; cross-user impact not demonstrated)
**Type:** DOM/Stored XSS candidate (self-XSS only as tested)
**Asset:** https://account.ui.com/requests/:ticketId
**Authentication:** user (test Account A/B)
**Severity:** Low (self-XSS; excluded from confirmed-vuln.txt)
**Tested at:** 2026-11-11T05:56Z

## Summary
The account SPA renders Zendesk comment bodies with `html-react-parser`, with no
client-side sanitizer present in the bundle. `html_body` is taken from
`GET https://account.svc.ui.com/zendesk/comments/{id}` and parsed to React elements.
If attacker-controlled HTML could be delivered into a ticket comment that a *different*
user/support agent views in this UI, it would be stored XSS. As tested, ticket comments
are strictly per-owner (cross-account access returns 403), so an injected payload only
executes in the attacker's own session, which is not reportable per AGENT.md §9.

## Evidence
- Bundle: `result/client-side/account.ui.com/login/javascript/index-BAUIMHQa.js`
  (SHA256 `29983e5e...855c18a`).
- Component `WLt` (~offset 6404335):
  `({author_type:t,...,html_body:i,attachments:a})=>... jsx(G,{children: BLt(i)})`
- `BLt` = bundled `html-react-parser` (`domToReact(htmlToDOM(html,...))`).
- Sanitizer grep: `DOMPurify`=0, `dompurify`=0, `sanitize-html`=0, `xss(`=0.
- Source endpoint path from SPA config: `zendeskComments: 'zendesk/comments'`
  on base `https://account.svc.ui.com`.

## Negative / limiting checks (from prior sessions)
- `GET /zendesk/comments/{id}` for another account's ticket -> `403 FORBIDDEN`.
- `POST /zendesk/comment/{ticketId}` requires `x-captcha-token`; write attempted only
  on the test account's own ticket.
- Zendesk normally sanitizes stored comment HTML server-side; not bypass-tested this run
  (no new authenticated session established).

## Why not confirmed
No delivery path to another user/support agent was found, and no JavaScript execution was
observed. Self-XSS without a delivery vector is excluded by AGENT.md §9.

`Confirmed-vuln.txt: excluded — self-XSS / no cross-user delivery, impact unresolved.`

## Remediation
Sanitize `html_body` before parsing (DOMPurify with an allowlist) or render as plain
text; enforce the same sanitization for any future admin/agent rendering of ticket HTML.

===== result/findings/cors-credentialed-reflection.md =====
# Candidate (UNRESOLVED) — Credentialed CORS reflection on sso.ui.com

**Status:** Confirmed misconfiguration; exploitation impact Unresolved (no attacker-controlled `*.ui.com` origin found).
**Severity if exploitable:** unknown.
**Host:** `https://sso.ui.com`
**Date:** 2026-11-10

## Summary
`sso.ui.com` reflects the request `Origin` in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` together with
`Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` whenever the origin's host ends in `.ui.com`.
This would allow a malicious page hosted on any `*.ui.com` subdomain to make
credentialed cross-origin reads of the victim's SSO API (profile, sessions, MFA list,
addresses, activity). No attacker-controlled `ui.com` subdomain was identified, so
this remains a defense-in-depth / trust-boundary observation rather than a confirmed vuln.

## Reproduction
```
curl -sSi https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/user/self \
  -H 'Origin: https://evil.ui.com' \
  -H "Cookie: UBIC_AUTH=<redacted>"
```
Response includes:
```
access-control-allow-origin: https://evil.ui.com
access-control-allow-credentials: true
vary: origin, Accept-Encoding, Origin
```
Preflight:
```
curl -sSi -X OPTIONS https://sso.ui.com/api/sso/v1/user/self \
  -H 'Origin: https://evil.ui.com' \
  -H 'Access-Control-Request-Method: PUT' \
  -H 'Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type'
```
allows `PUT, POST, PATCH, DELETE`, `content-type`, `max-age=600`.

## Negative checks
- `Origin: https://evil.com` -> no ACAO.
- `Origin: null` -> no ACAO.
- `Origin: https://account.ui.com.evil.com` -> no ACAO (suffix check is label-based).
- `Origin: https://evil.ubnt.com` -> not tested (out of scope; `ubnt.com` is out of scope).
- `account.svc.ui.com` rejects origins other than `https://account.ui.com` (403).

## Why not confirmed
Exploitation requires the attacker to control content on a `*.ui.com` origin
(user content, uploaded HTML, or a subdomain takeover). Wildcard DNS `*.ui.com` points to
AWS CloudFront and no such controllable origin was found. Without that, the reflected
CORS grants no capability beyond what the victim's own origin already has.

## Session 4 update (2026-11-10)
- Re-confirmed on `sso.ui.com`: `Origin: https://evil.ui.com` -> `ACAO: https://evil.ui.com`,
  `ACAC: true`; preflight allows `PUT, POST, PATCH, DELETE` with `content-type`.
  Arbitrary non-`ui.com` origins (`https://attacker.example`) and `null` are rejected.
- Also confirmed credentialed reflection on `cloudaccess.svc.ui.com`:
  `Origin: https://evil.ui.com` -> `ACAO: https://evil.ui.com`, `ACAC: true`,
  `Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET,HEAD,OPTIONS,PUT,POST,DELETE`. This service exposes
  host/backup/credential endpoints, so the latent impact is higher than the SSO API alone.
- Wildcard DNS `*.ui.com` resolves to AWS CloudFront. Random names return
  `302 https://account.ui.com/login?redirect=https%3A%2F%2F<host>%2F`; `/index.html`
  is served from S3 (HTTP 200, `text/html`) and loads the fixed "UniFi Site Manager"
  SPA from the same wildcard origin. Direct `PUT`/`POST`/`DELETE` to that origin return
  `403`; no attacker-writable object or Host-header reflection was found.
- Conclusion unchanged: the CORS policy trusts the entire `*.ui.com` namespace, but no
  attacker-controlled `*.ui.com` origin (stored HTML/JS, takeover, or writable bucket)
  was identified. Kept as Unresolved rather than a standalone finding.
